  <h1>Components, and how to use them</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    This site holds two things. A library of ready-made components you can put on a page today, and the
    manual for the plugin that runs them. Five chapters, one hour, and you can write your own.
  </p>

  <h2>What the plugin does</h2>

  <p>
    A component is a directory: a field definition, a piece of markup, optionally a stylesheet. A page
    stores only <em>which</em> components appear in which order and which values their fields hold — none
    of how they look.
  </p>

  <p>
    That one decision carries the rest. Change a component's <code>render.php</code> and every instance on
    every page changes with it. No migration, no search-and-replace, no "this only affects new pages".
  </p>

  <p>
    Fields come from <a href="https://wordpress.org/plugins/secure-custom-fields/">Secure Custom Fields</a>
    or Advanced Custom Fields. Storage and rendering are the plugin's own. Either host works; you need one
    of them active.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both are free for this. Advanced Custom Fields is enough in its free edition — the plugin asks the
    host only for single-value fields and renders lists, groups and rows itself, precisely because
    repeaters sit behind ACF's paid tier. Secure Custom Fields has them anyway. Nothing here needs a
    licence key, an account or a subscription: the plugin contains no network call at all.
  </p>

  <h2>In five minutes</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Install the plugin.</strong> Upload the ZIP under <em>Plugins → Add New → Upload Plugin</em>
        and activate it. A new menu entry <em>Page Builder</em> appears in the sidebar.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Open a page in the block editor</strong> and press <code>+</code>. The category
        <em>PhoenixSEO Components</em> is the first one in the inserter.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Place a component</strong>, for example <em>FAQ</em>. The block appears with its fields
        already filled from the example content, if the component ships any.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Select the block</strong> and switch the sidebar to its <em>Block</em> tab. The fields are
        there, not in a modal. Fill them in, press <em>Publish</em>.
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    That is the whole day-to-day workflow. Everything below is either a detail of it or a way out of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where components live</h2>

  <p>
    Four directories are scanned, in this order. The first match wins, so a component in the theme
    overrides one of the same type that ships with the plugin — and the plugin's own copy survives every
    update untouched.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Directory</th>
        <th>Belongs to</th>
        <th>Editable in wp-admin</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>wp-content/themes/&lt;child&gt;/sdc-components/</code></td>
        <td>the theme, child first</td>
        <td>no — read-only here</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>wp-content/themes/&lt;parent&gt;/sdc-components/</code></td>
        <td>the theme</td>
        <td>no — read-only here</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>wp-content/sdc-components/</code></td>
        <td>the site</td>
        <td>yes</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>wp-content/plugins/phoenixseo-page-builder/schemas/</code></td>
        <td>the plugin</td>
        <td>no — replaced on update</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p class="note">
    <strong>The one thing to remember</strong>
    A component that ships with the plugin or the theme cannot be edited in wp-admin, on purpose: the next
    deploy or update would overwrite the change without a word. Duplicate it into
    <code>wp-content/sdc-components/</code> and edit the copy.
  </p>

  <h2>The library</h2>

  <p>
    Ten components ship with the plugin, and every one of them is on this site with its rendered output,
    its fields and a ZIP of its directory. They are a course through the format: <em>hero</em> is a
    definition and nothing else, <em>pricing-table</em> nests a repeater inside a repeater,
    <em>vinyl</em> computes what nobody should type.
  </p>

